  "sourceNotes": "Chicken tikka masala is a dish that developed in British curry houses in the second half of the twentieth century. The origins are disputed between Glasgow and various restaurant kitchens in the 1970s. It became officially named Britain's favourite dish in a 2001 speech by Foreign Secretary Robin Cook. It combines the dry-heat cooking of Mughal tikka with a British preference for sauce, and is sometimes cited as an example of Anglo-Indian cooking.",
